import "core"


# DISABLED — a compiler gap, kept as the repro of it.
  A declaration naming a data type by its bare name gives every field of it [TopType], where a
  definition destructuring that same type narrows each field to the type it was declared with. So
  the declaration is never fulfilled and the program fails with:
    Declaration input type: /nameOf(/this=/Plain(/name=TopType, /other=TopType))
    Definition input type:  /nameOf(/this=/Plain(/name=/Data/String, /other=/Data/String))
    Undefined part: /nameOf(/this=(/Plain(/name=TopType - /Data/String, /other=TopType) + ...))
  It is the bare name that does it, not the field types: this fails the same way for two
  [/Data/Primitive/String] fields as for a [/Data/Maybe] one. It is not the receiver form either —
  written as @nameOf(d -> Plain) -> String@ with @nameOf(d= Plain(name, other))= name@ it fails
  identically.
  A class named the same way does not fail, which is why [/Data/List] carries [length] and
  [/Data/Algebra/Eq] carries [equal]: a class reference is not a partial carrying fields to be
  widened. So the gap is reached only by a data type, and only by one whose fields a definition
  reads.
data(Plain(name -> String, other -> String))

:Plain.nameOf -> String
/Plain(name, other).nameOf= name

main{io -> IO} =
  #assert(test= /Plain(name= "a", other= "b").nameOf == "a")
  io
